Found in the k125 review. A Hermes config with "mcp_servers: &m" (an anchored null) and a later "other: *m" passes every early check: childMapping (cmd/launcher/hermes.go around 438-445) replaces the anchored null with a fresh mapping and drops the anchor; only anchored mappings are refused (around 434). checkEdit then refuses with "the result would not parse: yaml: unknown anchor 'm' referenced", which does not tell the user what to change. Fix: refuse an anchored node of any kind in childMapping with the same kind of message as the anchored-mapping refusal. Note: k125's TestHermesSetupChecksTheEditBeforeWriting uses this input to reach checkEdit; whoever fixes this must give that test another input only checkEdit refuses (or argue that none is left and the safety net is pinned otherwise).

Fix (worker, 2026-11-21): childMapping (cmd/launcher/hermes.go) refuses any value that carries an anchor; before, it refused only an anchored mapping. The message is the one the anchored mapping already had: "mcp_servers carries the anchor &m; adding to it would change its aliases too; refusing to change it". The same function serves unreagent.local.yaml, so an anchored null mcp there now fails with "add the token to <path>: mcp carries the anchor &m; adding to it would change its aliases too (nothing written)". Doc comments of childMapping and mergeHermesServer updated. mappingEntry is untouched.

Tests (hermes_test.go): a new case "anchored null mcp_servers" in TestHermesSetupRefusesUnmergeableServers, and a new TestHermesSetupRefusesAnchoredNullLocalMCP. Both were red before the fix, on checkEdit's "the result would not parse: yaml: unknown anchor 'm' referenced". Both are green after it.

k125 obligation: TestHermesSetupChecksTheEditBeforeWriting now uses "system_prompt: >" with an indented list (a folded block scalar with more-indented lines), outside the entry. The node edit leaves it alone, but the vendored yaml.v3 v3.0.1 encoder writes it back with an extra blank line before the indented lines. Only checkEdit refuses it: "the result would change more than mcp_servers.unreagent". I chose an encoder round-trip bug over the other gaps I found, which sit in hermes.go itself (listed below) and are cards someone may close. The test comment says it rests on the encoder: a yaml.v3 upgrade, or a workaround, needs a new input there.

Mutations, on the final tree: dropping the checkEdit call in hermesSetup turns only TestHermesSetupChecksTheEditBeforeWriting red; hermes-setup then writes the changed prompt. Reverting the fix (anchor check on mappings only) turns only the two new tests red. The k125 test stays green, so it no longer depends on k134.

Check green: gofmt -l cmd internal skills is empty; go vet ./... and GOOS=windows go vet ./... pass; go test -count=1 ./... passes; go test -race -count=1 ./cmd/launcher passes; make linux and make windows build. Tests ran on Linux only. The change is platform-neutral YAML logic, and Windows was only cross-compiled, not run.

Drift for the dispatcher to file, all on this board:
(1) localConfigWithToken's own checkEdit call is not pinned. Dropping it leaves the suite green, at HEAD too (mutation-run). Lane: test-writer.
(2) hermes-setup refuses a Hermes config that holds, anywhere, a folded block scalar with a more-indented line, or a literal block scalar whose text starts with a blank line ("|" followed by an empty line). yaml.v3 v3.0.1 does not write either back as read. checkEdit catches it, but its message does not name the key or the cause. This is a user-visible limitation. Fixing it means TestHermesSetupChecksTheEditBeforeWriting needs another input.
(3) plainMergeKeys clears the !!merge tag of a quoted merge key (!!merge "<<"), so it is written back as a string key "<<". Only checkEdit catches it ("would change more than"). Contrived; low.
(4) An entry key spelled with a non-str tag (e.g. "!!binary dXJs" for url) is missed by mappingEntry. A second url is added, and the old one wins on decode. Only checkEdit catches it ("would not hold the new"). Contrived; low.

